1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,807
When we train an AI

2
00:00:01,807 --> 00:00:04,157
we often ask for something like this:

3
00:00:04,157 --> 00:00:05,681
be as helpful as you can

4
00:00:05,681 --> 00:00:07,904
but keep harm below a certain line.

5
00:00:08,254 --> 00:00:10,230
"Do your best" and "never cross

6
00:00:10,230 --> 00:00:12,397
this line" are different requests.

7
00:00:12,397 --> 00:00:14,246
How does a model follow both?

8
00:00:14,596 --> 00:00:16,289
Over two hundred years ago

9
00:00:16,289 --> 00:00:17,983
the mathematician Lagrange

10
00:00:17,983 --> 00:00:19,220
came up with a way.

11
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:21,565
It's called the Lagrange multiplier.

12
00:00:29,867 --> 00:00:31,824
Making one thing as good as possible

13
00:00:31,824 --> 00:00:33,617
without crossing a line is called

14
00:00:33,617 --> 00:00:34,976
constrained optimization.

15
00:00:35,326 --> 00:00:37,835
Lagrange's idea: don't build a wall.

16
00:00:37,835 --> 00:00:39,717
Put a price on crossing it.

17
00:00:40,067 --> 00:00:41,182
Go a little over

18
00:00:41,182 --> 00:00:42,994
and you lose a few points.

19
00:00:42,994 --> 00:00:45,572
That price is the Lagrange multiplier

20
00:00:45,572 --> 00:00:46,826
usually written λ.

21
00:00:47,176 --> 00:00:48,608
Say a parent allows one

22
00:00:48,608 --> 00:00:50,165
hour of video games a day

23
00:00:50,165 --> 00:00:52,344
and the kid keeps sneaking in more.

24
00:00:52,694 --> 00:00:54,880
So the parent tries a new deal:

25
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:56,573
every extra minute costs

26
00:00:56,573 --> 00:00:58,054
a dollar of allowance

27
00:00:58,054 --> 00:01:00,381
and every minute under earns one.

28
00:01:00,731 --> 00:01:01,965
Set the price too low

29
00:01:01,965 --> 00:01:03,668
and the kid goes over anyway.

30
00:01:03,668 --> 00:01:06,135
Too high, and he stops playing altogether.

31
00:01:06,485 --> 00:01:07,783
Get it just right

32
00:01:07,783 --> 00:01:09,234
and he stops around

33
00:01:09,234 --> 00:01:11,448
the one-hour mark on his own.

34
00:01:11,448 --> 00:01:13,510
That just-right price is λ.

35
00:01:17,633 --> 00:01:19,549
Let's try a small experiment.

36
00:01:19,549 --> 00:01:21,333
Here's a map of a mountain.

37
00:01:21,333 --> 00:01:23,117
Each ring is a contour line

38
00:01:23,117 --> 00:01:25,364
the same height all the way round.

39
00:01:25,714 --> 00:01:28,315
Our job is to find the highest spot.

40
00:01:28,665 --> 00:01:29,843
But there's a rule:

41
00:01:29,843 --> 00:01:31,952
stay inside the fence around camp.

42
00:01:31,952 --> 00:01:33,751
And the summit is outside it.

43
00:01:34,101 --> 00:01:35,738
So we do it Lagrange's way:

44
00:01:35,738 --> 00:01:36,889
take the fence down

45
00:01:36,889 --> 00:01:38,647
and charge λ points for every

46
00:01:38,647 --> 00:01:40,708
step away from the center of camp.

47
00:01:41,058 --> 00:01:43,422
Then find the spot with the most height

48
00:01:43,422 --> 00:01:44,756
left after the charge.

49
00:01:44,756 --> 00:01:45,847
This dot marks it.

50
00:01:46,197 --> 00:01:48,446
Start with a price of 0. No charge

51
00:01:48,446 --> 00:01:50,959
so the dot runs straight to the summit

52
00:01:50,959 --> 00:01:52,613
right out past the fence.

53
00:01:52,963 --> 00:01:54,432
So we raise the price

54
00:01:54,432 --> 00:01:56,811
and the farther outside the dot is

55
00:01:56,811 --> 00:01:58,280
the more we raise it.

56
00:01:58,630 --> 00:02:01,812
At a price of 1.5, the dot backs up a bit

57
00:02:01,812 --> 00:02:03,597
but it's still outside.

58
00:02:03,597 --> 00:02:06,235
Raise it again, it backs up again.

59
00:02:06,585 --> 00:02:09,208
At 3, the dot stops right on the fence.

60
00:02:09,558 --> 00:02:11,274
That's exactly the highest

61
00:02:11,274 --> 00:02:13,386
you can reach with the fence up:

62
00:02:13,386 --> 00:02:14,508
a height of 7.75.

63
00:02:14,858 --> 00:02:15,981
Push the price to 4

64
00:02:15,981 --> 00:02:18,285
and the dot pulls back inside the fence

65
00:02:18,285 --> 00:02:19,998
giving up height for nothing.

66
00:02:20,348 --> 00:02:22,143
Now look at that just-right point.

67
00:02:22,143 --> 00:02:23,411
The contour line through

68
00:02:23,411 --> 00:02:24,731
it just touches the fence

69
00:02:24,731 --> 00:02:26,104
so walking along the fence

70
00:02:26,104 --> 00:02:27,371
only takes you downhill.

71
00:02:27,721 --> 00:02:29,458
That's the Lagrange condition:

72
00:02:29,458 --> 00:02:30,384
at the best spot

73
00:02:30,384 --> 00:02:32,005
the steepest way uphill goes

74
00:02:32,005 --> 00:02:33,800
straight out through the fence.

75
00:02:37,933 --> 00:02:39,017
You might think λ

76
00:02:39,017 --> 00:02:40,610
is just a knob we turned.

77
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:42,204
But it also tells you how

78
00:02:42,204 --> 00:02:43,606
expensive the rule is.

79
00:02:43,956 --> 00:02:46,452
Make the fence a tiny bit bigger

80
00:02:46,452 --> 00:02:48,636
from a radius of 1.5 to 1.6.

81
00:02:48,986 --> 00:02:50,926
The highest you can reach

82
00:02:50,926 --> 00:02:52,711
goes from 7.75 to 8.04.

83
00:02:52,711 --> 00:02:54,883
That's a gain of about three

84
00:02:54,883 --> 00:02:56,668
times the extra radius.

85
00:02:57,018 --> 00:02:58,498
So a λ of three means:

86
00:02:58,498 --> 00:03:00,181
loosen the fence a little

87
00:03:00,181 --> 00:03:03,007
and you climb about three times that much.

88
00:03:03,357 --> 00:03:05,623
Economists call this a shadow price.

89
00:03:05,973 --> 00:03:06,973
And if the summit

90
00:03:06,973 --> 00:03:08,679
were already inside the fence

91
00:03:08,679 --> 00:03:09,561
λ would be zero

92
00:03:09,561 --> 00:03:11,032
because the rule wouldn't

93
00:03:11,032 --> 00:03:12,208
be holding you back.

94
00:03:12,558 --> 00:03:14,297
So λ tells you which rules

95
00:03:14,297 --> 00:03:16,169
are really pinning you down.

96
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:22,868
Back to where we started. In 2023

97
00:03:22,868 --> 00:03:25,670
a team at Peking University proposed

98
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:28,083
a way to train language models.

99
00:03:28,083 --> 00:03:30,029
They called it Safe RLHF.

100
00:03:30,379 --> 00:03:31,827
One scoring model rates

101
00:03:31,827 --> 00:03:33,338
how helpful an answer is

102
00:03:33,338 --> 00:03:34,597
another how harmful.

103
00:03:34,947 --> 00:03:36,975
We want harm to stay under the line

104
00:03:36,975 --> 00:03:38,772
and as much help as we can get.

105
00:03:39,122 --> 00:03:41,183
They used a Lagrange multiplier:

106
00:03:41,183 --> 00:03:42,857
take the helpfulness score

107
00:03:42,857 --> 00:03:45,176
and subtract λ times the harm score.

108
00:03:45,526 --> 00:03:48,011
And λ adjusts itself during training.

109
00:03:48,011 --> 00:03:50,631
When harm goes over the line, λ goes up

110
00:03:50,631 --> 00:03:52,445
and safety counts for more.

111
00:03:52,795 --> 00:03:55,400
When harm stays under, λ comes back down

112
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:57,679
and the model is free to help more.

113
00:03:57,679 --> 00:03:59,893
Just like the dot on our mountain.

114
00:04:00,243 --> 00:04:02,860
Prices on rules show up all over AI.

115
00:04:03,210 --> 00:04:06,224
Take regularization. It puts a price on complexity

116
00:04:06,224 --> 00:04:08,273
so the model doesn't just memorize

117
00:04:08,273 --> 00:04:10,021
the examples it's trained on.

118
00:04:10,371 --> 00:04:12,773
And chatbots often pay a price for drifting

119
00:04:12,773 --> 00:04:14,673
too far from their original model.

120
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:17,537
So, to sum it up:

121
00:04:17,537 --> 00:04:19,189
a Lagrange multiplier

122
00:04:19,189 --> 00:04:21,312
is a price on a constraint.

123
00:04:21,662 --> 00:04:23,135
Get the price just right

124
00:04:23,135 --> 00:04:24,731
and you don't need a wall.

125
00:04:24,731 --> 00:04:26,572
You'll stop right at the line.

126
00:04:26,922 --> 00:04:28,284
Next time you want it all

127
00:04:28,284 --> 00:04:30,955
but there's a line you can't cross, ask yourself:

128
00:04:30,955 --> 00:04:32,917
what is that line really costing me?

129
00:04:33,267 --> 00:04:35,163
Up next: gradient descent.
